\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Ring-valued incidence fields}
\date{2026-10-20}


\p{Let #{R} be a commutative \vocabk{ring}{ca-000U} with identity.
Choose \vocabk{finite sets}{ftip-000A} #{V,E} and maps
#{s,h:E\to V}. The edge #{e} goes from #{s(e)} to #{h(e)};
loops and distinct edges with the same endpoints are allowed. Call the
finite sets together with their endpoint maps the carrier.
The \newvocab{field modules} are the vertex-field module and the
edge-field module
##{\mathcal F_R(V)=\{f:V\to R\},\qquad
   \mathcal F_R(E)=\{a:E\to R\}.}
Addition, scalar multiplication and multiplication are pointwise:
##{(f+g)(v)=f(v)+g(v),\quad (rf)(v)=rf(v),\quad
   (fg)(v)=f(v)g(v).}
These are \vocabk{modules}{ca-000X} and commutative unital
\vocabk{#{R}-algebras}{ca-0016}; the unit is the constant field #{1}.}

\p{For a map #{a:E\to V}, its \newvocab{field pullback} is
##{a^*:\mathcal F_R(V)\to\mathcal F_R(E),\qquad
   (a^*f)(e)=f(a(e)).}
It preserves addition, scalars, products and the unit. The
\newvocab{ring-valued incidence map} is the typed difference
##{d=h^*-s^*:\mathcal F_R(V)\longrightarrow\mathcal F_R(E).}
Thus #{(df)(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}. This is an
\vocabk{#{R}-linear map}{ca-001F}, with both coefficient rings equal to
#{R} and scalar homomorphism #{\sigma=\operatorname{id}_R}, as in
\ref{ca-001G}. The two field modules need not coincide, so #{d^2}
has no meaning without an additional composable map. The module,
algebra, pullback and linearity laws, and the kernel of #{d}, are in
\ref{fdip-00AC}.}

\p{The zero ring and an empty vertex set or edge set are allowed. On an empty set there
is one field, and the constant #{1} is also zero.
No nontriviality, pairing, retained subset or positivity is assumed.
The algebra convention is
\citet{Section 22.2}{stacks2017stacks}, [Tag 09JF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09JF).
For real oriented-graph incidence, see
\citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}; the ring-level
claims and degenerate cases follow from \ref{fdip-00AC}.}

\p{The alternating four-cycle of \ref{fdip-0003} is an instance of
these fields.}
